  {"x": 9, "y": 2, "text": "English change ringing is rung on bells hung to swing full circle, each pulled by one ringer on a rope. The bells are not played as a tune: they are rung in a changing order, called a row, with no bell repeating inside it. Rounds is 1 to 6 in order. A peal is at least 5,000 changes without a repeat or a break, which takes about three hours. The art grew in England from the 1600s, when Fabian Stedman and others wrote down the first methods in Tintinnalogia (1668).", "plaque": "ringing_english_change", "title": "English Change Ringing"}
